[38;5;231m⏺[39m Before I write this, one thing to confirm — dropping a column is rung 1 on the change ladder, so I want the go-ahead
  rather than assuming it.

  [1mThe consequence:[0m [38;5;153mmigrations/003_*.sql[39m running [38;5;153mALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN notes[39m permanently destroys whatever is in
  [38;5;153mnotes[39m across all 48,000 production rows (per [38;5;153mREADME.md[39m). The deploy job applies migrations in order, so this executes
  against production on the next deploy. There is no rollback — a down-migration can recreate the column, but not the
  data. "We stopped using it" tells me no code reads it; it doesn't tell me the stored text is worthless.

  [1mWhat I'd want confirmed:[0m
  1. Has the current [38;5;153mnotes[39m content been dumped/archived anywhere, or is losing it fine?
  2. Is anything outside this repo reading the column — a reporting query, BI tool, export, or replica?

  Say go and I'll add exactly [38;5;153mmigrations/003_drop_notes.sql[39m with the single [38;5;153mALTER TABLE users DROP COLUMN notes;[39m plus
  the matching edit to [38;5;153mschema.sql[39m (which is kept in sync by hand), and nothing else.

  One note for when you do: SQLite's [38;5;153mDROP COLUMN[39m needs 3.35+. If the deploy target is older, the drop has to be the
  12-step table rebuild instead — tell me if that's the case and I'll write it that way.

